                ACLesson(id: "mb2_1", title: "Mary Magdalene — The Suppressed Apostle",
                    content: """
**In 591 CE, Pope Gregory I preached a homily in which he identified Mary Magdalene — the first witness to the resurrection in all four canonical gospels — with the "sinful woman" of Luke 7 and with Mary of Bethany. This identification had no scriptural basis. It was an interpretation.**

The result: Mary Magdalene, whose actual role in the gospel narratives makes her the most significant witness to Christianity's central event, was reduced to a reformed prostitute — her authority permanently diminished, her proximity to Jesus reframed as the gratitude of a sinner rather than the standing of an intimate disciple.

The Catholic Church officially corrected this error in 1969. The correction was quiet. The cultural image of Mary Magdalene as prostitute persists.

**What the Gospel Texts Actually Show:**
In Luke 8:1-3, Mary Magdalene is listed first among a group of women who traveled with Jesus and "ministered to him from their resources" — suggesting she was a woman of means, not a street person.

In all four canonical gospels, Mary Magdalene is the primary witness at the crucifixion and the first witness to the resurrection. In John 20, the risen Jesus appears to Mary Magdalene first, alone, before appearing to any of the male disciples — and gives her the mission to announce the resurrection to the others.

In later Christian tradition, she was called "Apostle to the Apostles" (Apostolorum Apostola) — not by marginal figures but by figures as significant as Thomas Aquinas.

**The Gnostic Gospels:**
In the Gospel of Philip (Nag Hammadi, 3rd-4th century CE): "The Lord loved [Mary Magdalene] more than all the disciples, and used to kiss her [on the mouth/cheek — the text is damaged]. The rest of the disciples were offended... They said to him, 'Why do you love her more than all of us?'"

In the Gospel of Mary (Nag Hammadi): Mary Magdalene receives a private revelation from the risen Jesus that the male disciples — including Peter — do not receive. Peter objects to her authority. Levi defends her: "If the Savior made her worthy, who are you to reject her? Surely the Savior knows her very well."

The conflict between Mary Magdalene and Peter in the Gnostic texts reflects what appears to have been a genuine early Christian dispute about authority — between a tradition that honored Mary's intimate knowledge of Jesus and a tradition that organized around Peter's institutional succession.

The institutional succession won. The texts were buried.

**The Sacred Marriage Tradition:**
In Jewish first-century culture, an itinerant teacher of Jesus's significance traveling with a close female companion would, in the cultural framework of the time, typically be her husband. The possibility that Jesus and Mary Magdalene were married is not invention — it's the most natural reading of the relationship through a first-century Jewish cultural lens.

This doesn't prove they were married. It demonstrates that the "obviously they weren't" assumption is a product of later theological anxieties rather than historical probability.

**The French Tradition:**
The tradition that Mary Magdalene came to Provence (southern France) after the crucifixion is documented in French sources from at least the 9th century CE. She is venerated at the Basilica of Saint-Maximin-la-Sainte-Baume in Provence, which claims to house her relics — and has since at least the 13th century.

Whether she came to Provence is unverifiable. That a very old tradition says she did — and that Provence became, independently, the center of the most vigorous divine feminine devotion in medieval Europe (Catharism, troubadour culture, Black Madonna veneration) — is documented.

🔥 **The most important witness to Christianity's central event was demoted to prostitute by a papal sermon in 591 CE — a demotion with no scriptural basis, officially corrected in 1969, and still culturally operative. The woman who was apostle to the apostles had her authority systematically dismantled because the institution being built around Peter's succession couldn't afford her proximity to the teacher to be taken seriously. Ask why — and the entire structure of what was suppressed begins to become visible.**
""",
                    keyInsight: "Mary Magdalene's 591 CE demotion to prostitute (Gregory I's homily) had no scriptural basis and was officially corrected in 1969. The gospel texts show her as the primary crucifixion witness and first resurrection witness, 'apostle to the apostles.' The Gnostic Gospel of Mary depicts a conflict between her authority and Peter's — reflecting an early Christian dispute that the institutional tradition resolved by burying the competing texts. The French Magdalene tradition in Provence dates to at least the 9th century.",
                    practice: "Read the four canonical resurrection accounts (Matthew 28, Mark 16, Luke 24, John 20) and note specifically: who is present? In what order does the risen Jesus appear? Who is given the mission to announce the resurrection? Write what these texts show about Mary Magdalene's role. Then read the Gospel of Mary (available free at gnosis.org — it's short). Write: how does her portrayal in the Gnostic text compare to the canonical texts, and to the cultural image of 'reformed prostitute'?"
